## Decision

This record replaces [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject), which remains unchanged
as the historical record. The old subject-only rule merges different bugs and separates duplicates.
Read the report body, look up the tenant's locale and booking-form version, and use the channel
to identify the failing path. Merge only when the evidence supports the same cause.

<Callout kind="warn">
The “one issue, one fix” summary of #4471 is incomplete. The rendered evidence separates a parser
problem from a booking-form hint problem. A parser change alone will leave the form problem open.
</Callout>

## Separate engineering tickets

**Parser — month-first parsing of day-first dates.** The shared parser reads dates month first.
For example, R-41200 describes 7 April becoming 4 July at harbour.dental through the app.
Use the tenant's actual locale rather than the reporter's browser locale. Make parsing explicit
and locale-aware, then verify the resulting invite through app and email paths and the older booking form.

**Hint — booking-v3 prints the wrong format.** For day-first tenants the form prints `MM/DD/YYYY`,
while its reader interprets input in the tenant's order. For example, R-41241 at alder.physio came
through the form and describes people arriving on 8 June for 6 August. Align the hint and reader
with the tenant locale and verify the complete input-to-invite path. This needs its own ticket and fix.

The live table computes each ticket's report count from unique IDs, tenant configuration and channel.
Intake tags remain visible as evidence of how reports were first classified; they are not cause labels.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The “no ticket of its own” label refers to the exported desk queue. The separate GitHub tickets
record this split for engineering; they do not alter the historical export.

## Counting, timing and uncertainty

<IntakeEvidence />

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

## Working rule for the desk

1. Deduplicate repeated export rows by report ID. Two customers reporting the same failure still count as two reports.
2. Read the body and look up the tenant. Confirm the input path, locale and form version; do not infer them from a subject or browser language.
3. Use `causeOf` and `sameCause` in `src/lib/kb/causes.ts` as the current routing aid. Matching subjects alone never establish a duplicate.
4. Keep different causes in separate engineering tickets even when the subjects match. Merge reports with different subjects when the same failure is supported.
5. An unknown tenant or unexplained symptom requires investigation. The explorer's “keep apart” result for unknown tenants means insufficient evidence, not a proven new cause.
6. Keep unresolved bodies visible and reclassify them when new evidence arrives. Rebuild the KB when the desk exports new reports or tenant settings.

## Check the rule in the rendered view

The explorer runs the shared cause logic. App and email reports from the same day-first tenant
can share the parser cause despite different subjects. A booking-v3 form report and an app report
from the same tenant have different causes even when their subjects match. Matching form subjects
across booking-v3 and booking-v2 tenants also stay apart. Unknown tenants are not auto-merged.

<DedupeExplorer />

## Keep the knowledge base current

The triage page now points to this rule. The original decision stays readable, and the incident
page points here rather than presenting its former summary as current guidance. Review dates in
the index are evidence of maintenance, not proof that a claim agrees with its live tables.
Reopen this decision if the reports or tenant settings change the cause split.

